+        // os8088: ONE interrupt, at completion - which is what a 765 raises
+        // for a RECALIBRATE and what operation_seek already sends. Upstream
+        // raised a second one HERE, at command time, so every recalibrate
+        // cost the guest two IRQ6s against the BIOS's one WAIT_INT. The spare
+        // one leaves the BDA's working-interrupt flag (0040:003E bit 7) SET,
+        // so the NEXT int 13h's wait returns at once and the BIOS polls the
+        // result phase while the transfer is still running: its 5-tick RQM
+        // timeout then answers 80h for any transfer that takes longer, which
+        // is every multi-sector read that waits most of a revolution for its
+        // first sector. A reset clears the calibrated bits, so the retry
+        // recalibrates and is poisoned again - three strikes, every time,
+        // under GLaBIOS. The IBM 27 OCT 82 ROM sees the same stale flag and
+        // survives it (it seeks first, and its result wait outlasts a
+        // revolution), so there it is silent - the accounting is still one
+        // interrupt out of step, which is not a machine anyone owns.
         self.op_charge(seek_us, true);
